{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01EVku5GTPVLVrYGR8wy96vp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants me to define three tiers (best/median/worst) for a \"Dream simulation\" based on the research project context. This is about the gold set validation experiment (650 windows, human-annotated) which is the GO/NO-GO gate for the paper.\n\nLet me analyze the key aspects:\n\n1. The paper's primary claim is \"Structural Features Dominate Zero-Shot Cross-Agent Transfer\"\n2. The gold set (650 windows, κ≥0.75) is the critical validation - it determines whether the structural features are detecting REAL stagnation vs. just matching circular LF labels\n3. D053 补缺实验 have been completed\n\n\n4. Now I need to check whether the gold-validated results still support EMNLP Findings acceptance by examining how structural features, probe, and LLM-as-judge perform against human ground truth labels, and whether the structural dominance gap holds up under this more rigorous evaluation.\n\nLooking at the dry run metrics where majority vote served as a proxy for gold, structural methods achieved near-perfect F1 scores around 0.997 while the GRU probe reached 0.964, but the real test is whether human annotations diverge significantly from majority vote and whether structural still maintains its advantage. The critical unknowns are whether human annotators agree with majority vote at high rates, whether structural features continue to outperform the probe on actual human labels, and whether the zero-shot cross-agent advantage persists when evaluated against true ground truth.\n\nI'm mapping out three scenarios based on human-majority vote agreement: best case where they align above 90% and structural dominance holds, median case with 75-90% agreement where the gap narrows but structural still wins, and worst case below 75% where the structural advantage might be an artifact of the majority vote setup. To track this, I need to measure the agreement rate between human and majority vote labels, compare structural and probe performance on human-annotated data, quantify the performance gap between them, and check whether the cross-agent structural advantage survives this evaluation.\n\nNow I'm thinking through what the gold set actually tells us. The core question is whether human labels validate that majority vote was a reasonable proxy for real stagnation, and whether structural features still outperform on genuinely labeled data. The circular concern is that structural features might just be reconstructing the labeling function logic rather than detecting actual stagnation—but if human labels agree with majority vote, that suggests the original labels captured something real, and structural's ability to match them means they're detecting genuine patterns. Conversely, if humans disagree substantially with majority vote, it means the labels were noisy and structural was just fitting that noise.\n\nI'm working through the expected outcomes: if gold validates majority vote, structural should stay high while probe drops relatively, maintaining the gap. If gold partially diverges, both drop but structural drops more since it's closer to the labeling function logic. In the worst case where gold significantly disagrees, structural collapses while probe holds up better because it captures semantic signals beyond the rule-based patterns. Now I'm considering the three ablation experiments for D053 that could strengthen the paper: the few-shot LLM judge comparison would show structural's advantage persists even with improved baselines, removing overlapping features would isolate whether the structural approach's gains are truly independent rather than circular, and instance isolation would validate that the method works beyond just memorizing agent patterns.\n\nThe best case is all three experiments confirm structural's robustness, the median case shows mixed results, and the worst case would be if few-shot closes the gap, ablation removes the advantage, or instance leakage is found. Now I need to build out the YAML with internally consistent numbers, but first I should reconsider the gold majority vote agreement metric—the dry run showed perfect agreement because it used MV as gold, but with real annotations I need to think about what percentage of the 650 windows actually match the majority vote label, and how that relates to the inter-annotator agreement.\n\nLooking at the cross-agent claim more carefully, it's evaluated on out-of-domain data while the gold set comes from in-domain SWE-Agent, so I can't directly validate the cross-agent claim against gold unless there's also out-of-domain gold available. But the context says all methods have been evaluated on the 650 gold windows with κ≥0.75, so the real question is whether the performance gaps and ordering hold up against true human labels rather than majority vote. If the probe achieved 0.9055 on the confidence≥2 test set against MV labels, it might drop to around 0.85 when evaluated against actual gold labels, assuming gold agrees with MV roughly 90% of the time.\n\nThe structural method is more concerning because it achieved 0.9951 on MV labels—essentially perfect alignment—which means any divergence between gold and MV directly tanks its gold F1, probably down to 0.90-0.92. This reveals that structural was overfitting to the MV labels themselves rather than learning genuine patterns, while the probe at least captured some real semantic signal that might generalize better. The cross-agent zero-shot gap I'm trying to validate is substantial—structural GB reaches 0.967 versus probe's 0.598 on MV data, a 36.9 point difference—but I need to see if this gap persists when both are measured against gold instead.\n\nNow I'm identifying the key metrics that will determine how to tier these results: the gold-MV agreement rate, how each method performs on gold labels, whether the in-domain gap holds up, what happens when I remove features that overlap with the labeling function logic, and whether few-shot prompting can close the gap between methods.\n\nFor the best-case scenario, I'm looking at gold-MV agreement above 90% to confirm the majority vote is reliable, structural methods hitting 93%+ on gold to prove they're capturing real stagnation rather than artifacts, a 7+ percentage point gap between structural and probe methods on gold data, and the overlap ablation showing 85%+ performance to demonstrate the structural signal isn't just echoing the labeling function. The few-shot LLM baseline staying below 75% would reinforce that these methods have genuine advantages.\n\nFor the median case, I'm expecting gold-MV agreement in the 82-89% range with some label noise, and structural performance around 85% on gold.\n\nFor the worst case, the numbers degrade substantially—gold-MV agreement drops to 72-81%, structural performance falls to 74-84% on gold, and the probe method stays in the 78-85% range. This scenario would confirm that the circular evaluation problem is real and that the majority vote labels themselves contain significant noise that undermines the validation.\n\nThe key insight across all scenarios is that I need to calibrate my claims based on how much the performance drops when moving from the labeling function evaluation to gold annotations. If the gap is small, I can confidently claim structural methods detect real stagnation. If it's large, I should soften the language around \"dominance\" and instead emphasize the practical value of these methods while acknowledging the gold-validated limitations.\n\nFor the worst case specifically, I'm reconsidering the framing entirely—rather than claiming structural methods are superior, I could position this as a methodological contribution about how weak supervision evaluation can be misleading. The paper becomes an argument that standard weak supervision benchmarks give a false picture of method performance, and that semantic representations actually generalize better to ground truth than structural proxies do. This keeps it at EMNLP level by focusing on the evaluation methodology itself rather than downgrading to a workshop paper.\n\nNow I'm mapping out what each experiment tier tells me about this narrative. For the ablation removing overlap features, the best case would show structural still works well without the circular signal, but the worst case—where performance drops to 0.65-0.74—suggests most of the structural gain was actually coming from that overlap leakage. The few-shot LLM baseline is critical here: if it reaches 0.80 or higher on gold, that significantly undermines my efficiency argument and closes the gap I'm trying to establish. For the cross-agent isolation check, I need to verify there's no data leakage between agents, with the best case showing the gold benchmark stays above 0.95 and the median case showing some degradation.\n\nEven if the experiments don't all go perfectly, I have a solid foundation for Findings: the gold annotation resource itself is valuable, the methodological insight about circular evaluation risks in weak supervision is a real contribution, and I can still make claims about probe robustness and temporal dynamics that hold up regardless of the ablation outcomes.\n\nNow I need to make sure the metrics are internally consistent across the best, median, and worst-case scenarios.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":37985,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":37985},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"39d53c5c-97d6-4d12-a8e5-def01d93be05","uuid":"e1d4c3bf-a4cc-4b09-935c-9f6f44d99b2b"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01EVku5GTPVLVrYGR8wy96vp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```yaml\ntiers:\n  best:\n    meaning: \"Gold 标注高度验证 MV 标签质量，structural dominance claim 在 ground truth 上成立，D053 补缺实验全部正向\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"Gold-MV agreement ≥90%：人工标注者对 stagnation 的判断与 LF majority vote 高度一致，证明 LF pipeline 标签可靠\"\n      - \"Structural 在 gold 上仍大幅领先 probe：circular eval 虽存在但 structural 检测的是真实停滞信号\"\n      - \"Overlap ablation 移除 LF 重叠特征后残差 F1 仍 ≥0.85：structural signal 有独立于 LF 逻辑的信息\"\n      - \"LLM-judge few-shot 仍 ≤0.75：efficiency advantage 在 gold 上依然成立\"\n      - \"Cross-agent instance isolation 后 GB ≥0.95：无信息泄露，跨域数字可信\"\n    number_ranges:\n      gold_mv_agreement_rate: [0.90, 0.95]\n      structural_rf_gold_macro_f1: [0.93, 0.96]\n      structural_gb_gold_macro_f1: [0.93, 0.96]\n      probe_gold_macro_f1: [0.83, 0.88]\n      structural_minus_probe_gold_pp: [7, 12]\n      llm_judge_fewshot_gold_f1: [0.68, 0.75]\n      overlap_ablation_residual_f1: [0.85, 0.92]\n      cross_agent_gb_instance_isolated: [0.95, 0.97]\n      gold_kappa: [0.78, 0.85]\n    narrative_direction: \"原始 framing 完全成立。Gold validation 成为论文的杀手级证据：'Human annotations confirm that structural features detect genuine stagnation — the 36.9pp zero-shot advantage is not a circular artifact but reflects a real signal extraction efficiency gap.' Claim 1 用 gold F1 替代 LF F1 作为 headline number（稍低但 ground-truth validated），dominance claim 无需调整措辞。Circular evaluation 从 'concern' 降级为 'caveat we addressed'。整体叙事更强——从 'interesting finding with circularity caveat' 升级为 'gold-validated practical guidance'。EMNLP main 有竞争力。\"\n\n  median:\n    meaning: \"Gold 部分验证 MV 标签——大部分一致但在语义多样停滞上存在系统性分歧；structural 仍优于 probe 但 'dominance' 需降级为 'advantage'\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"Gold-MV agreement 82-89%：annotators 在明显停滞/明显进步上与 MV 一致，但对语义多样停滞（FP 高置信区）判断不同\"\n      - \"Structural 在 gold 上仍优于 probe 但差距缩至 2-6pp：structural 的 circular 部分被 gold 惩罚，但跨域优势仍在\"\n      - \"Overlap ablation 残差 F1 0.75-0.84：有部分非 circular 信号，但不足以单独支撑 dominance claim\"\n      - \"LLM-judge few-shot 达到 0.72-0.80：缩小差距但仍有成本-性能 tradeoff argument\"\n      - \"Cross-agent instance isolation 后 GB 降至 0.92-0.95：微量 leakage 效应存在但不 invalidate 结论\"\n    number_ranges:\n      gold_mv_agreement_rate: [0.82, 0.89]\n      structural_rf_gold_macro_f1: [0.85, 0.92]\n      structural_gb_gold_macro_f1: [0.86, 0.92]\n      probe_gold_macro_f1: [0.80, 0.87]\n      structural_minus_probe_gold_pp: [2, 6]\n      llm_judge_fewshot_gold_f1: [0.72, 0.80]\n      overlap_ablation_residual_f1: [0.75, 0.84]\n      cross_agent_gb_instance_isolated: [0.92, 0.95]\n      gold_kappa: [0.75, 0.80]\n    narrative_direction: \"Claim 1 措辞从 'dominance' 调整为 'practical advantage in zero-data deployment'——gold 验证 structural 仍是最佳零数据选择，但优势幅度需按 gold 数字重新量化（从 +36.9pp 降至 gold-validated gap ~+15-25pp in zero-shot cross-agent regime, estimated from in-domain gap scaling）。论文增加一个 subsection：'Gold Annotation Reveals LF Label Noise Patterns'，将 MV-gold 分歧作为额外分析贡献（哪类窗口 LF 标错、为什么）。Circular evaluation 从 caveat 升级为 finding：'Structural features' in-domain advantage is partially inflated by circular evaluation; gold-validated gap is X pp vs LF-evaluated Y pp.' Probe 的 narrative 加强——'semantic probe demonstrates robustness to label noise, maintaining performance on gold despite training on noisy LF labels.' TLM negative finding 不受影响。整体仍是 solid EMNLP Findings paper，但 headline 数字更保守，且需要补一个 gold-based error analysis section (~0.5 page)。\"\n\n  worst:\n    meaning: \"Gold 显著偏离 MV——structural 'dominance' 被证实是 circular artifact，probe 在 gold 上追平或反超 structural（in-domain），需要完全 reframe\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"Gold-MV agreement 72-81%：LF pipeline 在 ~20-28% 窗口上标签与人类不一致，尤其是语义多样停滞被 LF 漏标为 progress\"\n      - \"Structural 在 gold 上 F1 大幅下降且不再领先 probe：structural 本质是学习 LF 逻辑，当 LF 错时 structural 也错\"\n      - \"Probe 在 gold 上表现与 structural 持平或更优：semantic representation 对 gold-truth 更 robust（probe 的 FP 模式恰好是 gold 认为正确的）\"\n      - \"Overlap ablation 残差 F1 < 0.75：移除 LF 重叠特征后 structural 严重退化，证实 circular 是主要信号源\"\n      - \"LLM-judge few-shot 在 gold 上达到 0.78-0.85：与 structural 持平，efficiency argument 被削弱\"\n    number_ranges:\n      gold_mv_agreement_rate: [0.72, 0.81]\n      structural_rf_gold_macro_f1: [0.74, 0.84]\n      structural_gb_gold_macro_f1: [0.75, 0.85]\n      probe_gold_macro_f1: [0.78, 0.85]\n      structural_minus_probe_gold_pp: [-4, 2]\n      llm_judge_fewshot_gold_f1: [0.78, 0.85]\n      overlap_ablation_residual_f1: [0.65, 0.74]\n      cross_agent_gb_instance_isolated: [0.87, 0.92]\n      gold_kappa: [0.75, 0.78]\n    narrative_direction: \"Core reframe：从 'structural dominance' 转向 'circular evaluation as a systematic risk in weak supervision for sequential data'。新 Claim 1 (primary)：Gold validation exposes that LF-derived evaluation systematically overestimates structural methods — a methodological warning for the WS community applying data programming to sequential/temporal domains. 新 Claim 2：Semantic probes trained on noisy LF labels show surprising robustness to label noise when evaluated on ground truth, suggesting embedding-based detectors learn signal beyond LF heuristics（probe 的 error pattern 与 gold 更 aligned）。Claim 3 (TLM negative) 不变。Claim 2-old (temporal dynamics) 缩为 supporting analysis。论文变为 methodology-oriented empirical study：(1) 量化 circular evaluation 的危害 + gold 作为解决方案的必要性；(2) 提供 650-window annotated benchmark 作为 resource contribution；(3) 方法选型结论反转——有 ground truth 时 probe 更可靠，structural 的 'advantage' 仅在无 gold 验证时的 proxy metric 上成立。仍冲 EMNLP Findings（analysis/methodology paper + resource），但 reviewer pitch 完全不同——从 'practical deployment guidance' 转为 'evaluation methodology contribution'。风险：reviewer 认为 '这只是说你们之前的评估有问题' 缺乏 novelty——对策是强调 systematic quantification + benchmark resource + 对 WS-on-sequential-data 的普适指导。\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":37985,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":37985},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"39d53c5c-97d6-4d12-a8e5-def01d93be05","uuid":"98b19c93-f950-45bb-a478-e41a968d3114"}
